# Kip Smudgewick's camp talks (story/camp_talk.gd): offered after a Long Rest once the opening `if` holds; each plays
# once. Quest: The Fine Print (narrative/companions/fine_print.dialogue). Mister Quillon calls at the fire twice:
# once to remind Kip of his terms, and once, if Kip has Strahd's signature, to be paid. The file offers its first talk
# that holds: when he's Strained or Doubtful (story/approval.gd), those first; then the quest's; then Warm, Close and
# Devoted.

# --- When he's lost faith in the party (approval) ----------------------------------------------------------------

~ talk_strained
if name:kip_smudgewick and approval.kip_smudgewick <= strained
-> strained
endif
-> END

~ strained
Narrator: Kip doesn't offer anyone an apricot tonight. That has never happened before. He sits with his hat in his lap and talks slowly, politely and very clearly, like a man reading out terms.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: I'd like it on the record, please, that I've been very patient. I'm a patient man. You have to be, with trees.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: But I've sat across a table from a devil, and I know what it looks like when somebody stops counting what things cost other people. I've seen it in a mirror. I don't want to see it in you.
if flag.dark_gifts_taken >= 1
interject name:kip_smudgewick: We let something in the amber give one of us a gift. Do you know what they call a gift, where Mister Quillon comes from? An advance.
endif
interject name:kip_smudgewick: When I settle with Mister Quillon, I'll do it on my own, if it comes to that. I'll not have him see me standing with people like this. He'd only take notes.
* Then hold us to account, Kip. -> strained_account
* We're sorry. -> strained_sorry
* Nobody asked you. -> strained_cold

~ strained_account
interject name:kip_smudgewick: I can do that. I'm very good at accounts. I'll keep a ledger. Two columns. The second one's for putting things right.
approve kip_smudgewick +10: You asked me to hold you to account
-> END

~ strained_sorry
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Accepted. Provisionally. Subject to terms. I'm sorry, I can't help it. Accepted.
approve kip_smudgewick +5: You told me you were sorry
-> END

~ strained_cold
Narrator: He puts his hat on, carefully, and pulls it down over his horns, and doesn't say anything else all night.
approve kip_smudgewick -6: You told me nobody asked me
-> END

~ talk_doubt
if name:kip_smudgewick and approval.kip_smudgewick == doubtful
-> doubt
endif
-> END

~ doubt
Narrator: Kip is doing his accounts tonight. You can tell because he keeps sighing at a little book and licking his pencil. Eventually he shuts it.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Can I raise a matter? Under any other business. It's a bit awkward. I'll be brief. I'm never brief.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: I've done a lot of deals in my life, and only one bad one. And lately, watching us, I keep getting the feeling I had in the lane that night. The polite feeling. The "sign here, it's all perfectly fair" feeling.
if flag.bella_pact
interject name:kip_smudgewick: We shook on it with a hag. I know what a handshake like that's worth. I've got one in my pocket.
endif
interject name:kip_smudgewick: I'm not saying anything. I'm just saying it. Notwithstanding.
* You're right. We'll be more careful. -> doubt_careful
* Every deal's a risk, Kip. -> doubt_risk
* Mind your own ledger. -> doubt_ledger

~ doubt_careful
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Lovely. Thank you. That's all I wanted. Apricot? I've got loads. I always have loads. That's sort of the problem.
approve kip_smudgewick +6: You said we'd be more careful
-> END

~ doubt_risk
interject name:kip_smudgewick: It is. That's true. Mum says you weigh the risk on one hand and the fruit on the other, and you never put your soul on the scale. I did. I'm saying don't.
approve kip_smudgewick +1: You told me every deal's a risk
-> END

~ doubt_ledger
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Right. Yes. Sorry. I'll mind it. ...I'll mind it very carefully.
approve kip_smudgewick -4: You told me to mind my own ledger
-> END

# --- His quest ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

~ talk_quillon
if name:kip_smudgewick and quest.the_fine_print == the_contract
-> quillon
endif
-> END

~ quillon
Narrator: Kip is sitting very close to the fire, closer than is comfortable, with his straw hat pulled down over his horns.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Did anyone else... has anyone else seen a man? In a grey coat? Just there? At the edge of the light?
Narrator: There's nobody at the edge of the light. Then there is: a slim gentleman in a grey coat and grey gloves, a ledger under his arm, standing exactly where the firelight stops as if it were a line painted on a floor.
Mister Quillon: Good evening. Please, nobody get up. Mister Smudgewick. I happened to be passing.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Nobody happens to be passing here. There's no here to pass. It's surrounded by fog.
Mister Quillon: Quite so. I travel on a pass. The lord of this land is very particular about his border, and I am very particular about manners, so I shan't stay. I only called to see how you were getting on.
Mister Quillon: Our agreement runs to the end of the final harvest. I merely mention it. And the way out I so generously offered, a signature from the lord of this land, remains on the table. I do hope you're making progress. Truly. I hate a default. The paperwork.
* Leave him alone. -> quillon_leave
* What happens if he can't pay? -> quillon_terms
* (Say nothing.) -> quillon_go

~ quillon_leave
Mister Quillon: Of course. I can't do anything else here, you understand; my jurisdiction ends at the mists. I can only talk. You'd be amazed what talking does.
approve kip_smudgewick +3: You told Mister Quillon to leave me alone
-> quillon_go

~ quillon_terms
Mister Quillon: Completion. The consideration is collected, the orchard continues to flourish, and his family never learns why. Everybody keeps something. It's a very fair contract. I drafted it myself.
-> quillon_go

~ quillon_go
Narrator: Then he isn't there. The firelight runs out to the dark and stops, and there's nobody standing where it stops.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Sorry. Sorry about that. He does that. He's very polite. That's the worst part. He's so polite.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Apricot? I need to be doing something with my hands.
if name:liriel_dawnsong
interject name:liriel_dawnsong: Kip. Whatever you signed, there is nothing in any contract that the Morninglord can't see through. We'll get you out.
endif
if name:godrick_pendlebrook
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Next time he comes, I'll fight him. Is he the fighting sort? He looked like the fighting sort. Thin ones are.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Please don't fight the devil, Godrick. It's in the contract. I think. I haven't read that bit.
endif
quest the_fine_print the_assessor
-> END

~ talk_reading
if name:kip_smudgewick and quest.the_fine_print == the_assessor
-> reading
endif
-> END

~ reading
Narrator: Kip has the contract out tonight, unrolled across his knees and over the log and onto the grass. It's longer unrolled than it could possibly be rolled. The ink moves a little in the firelight, like something asleep.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Mum always says read the small print. I never have. I signed it in the lane in the dark, crying. I'm going to read it now. Will somebody help? Some of the words are in a language that hurts.
if name:ratatoille
interject name:ratatoille: Give it here. It's like a recipe in a bad cookbook: all the important bits are in the method. Here. The way out. "A signature of the lord of the land of mists, freely given, in his own hand."
-> reading_found
elif name:wren_featherfoot
interject name:wren_featherfoot: Shove over. I've read a lot of contracts. Mostly upside down, across a desk, while someone wasn't looking. Here. "A signature of the lord of the land of mists, freely given, in his own hand."
-> reading_found
elif name:liriel_dawnsong
interject name:liriel_dawnsong: Let me. The temple taught us to read the old law-tongue; it's mostly for marriage settlements. Here. "A signature of the lord of the land of mists, freely given, in his own hand."
-> reading_found
endif
* [name:kip_smudgewick] [Investigation DC 14] Read it properly, all the way through. -> reading_self | reading_fail
* We'll find someone who reads contracts. -> reading_later

~ reading_self
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Hang on. Hang on. "A signature of the lord of the land of mists, freely given, in his own hand." That's it. That's the whole clause.
-> reading_found

~ reading_found
interject name:kip_smudgewick: A signature. He said "a signature". I thought he meant... I thought it meant his soul. On a contract. Signed over. I thought it was impossible. That's why he offered it.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: It doesn't say that. It doesn't say soul. It doesn't say contract. It says signature. Any signature.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: He wrote it sloppy. Mister Quillon wrote it sloppy because he never thought I'd get anywhere near a vampire lord. Or read.
if name:godrick_pendlebrook
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: So we just need the vampire to write his name on something. That's easy! People write their names on things all the time. I can do it now. Nearly.
endif
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Freely given. In his own hand. Right. Right. I can do that. I'm a salesman. Getting people to sign things is the entire job.
quest the_fine_print the_small_print
approve kip_smudgewick +3: You read the small print with me
-> END

~ reading_fail
interject name:kip_smudgewick: It keeps... the words keep moving. Every time I get to the end of a line it's a different line. I can't. I can't read it on my own.
-> reading_later

~ reading_later
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Somebody who reads contracts for a living. A lawyer. A scholar. Somebody who isn't scared of it. Somebody very boring.
-> END

~ talk_settle
# Approval (story/approval.gd): Strained or worse, and he won't call Quillon with the party watching until it's mended;
# Close or better, and the first apricot pit of a free orchard becomes a charm that works.
if name:kip_smudgewick and quest.the_fine_print >= the_small_print and not (quest.the_fine_print >= free) and (flag.strahd_letter_kept or flag.kip_invitation_kept or flag.kip_strahd_signed) and not (approval.kip_smudgewick <= strained)
-> settle
endif
-> END

~ settle
if not (quest.the_fine_print >= signed)
quest the_fine_print signed
endif
Narrator: Kip doesn't sit down tonight. He stands at the edge of the firelight with his hat in his hands, facing out into the dark.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Mister Quillon. If you'd be so kind. I'd like to settle my account.
Narrator: A pause. Then the gentleman in grey is standing just where the light stops, ledger under his arm, gloves buttoned.
Mister Quillon: Mister Smudgewick. You've been busy. I can smell the castle on you.
if flag.kip_strahd_signed
interject name:kip_smudgewick: A signature of the lord of the land of mists. Freely given. In his own hand. On the back of a bill for one apricot.
elif flag.kip_invitation_kept
interject name:kip_smudgewick: A signature of the lord of the land of mists. Freely given. In his own hand. At the bottom of a dinner invitation.
else
interject name:kip_smudgewick: A signature of the lord of the land of mists. Freely given. In his own hand. At the bottom of a letter he wrote us, to say hello.
endif
Narrator: Mister Quillon takes the paper between two grey fingers. He reads it. He opens his ledger and reads his own contract, once, twice, three times. His face goes a colour you have never seen on a face.
Mister Quillon: "A signature."
interject name:kip_smudgewick: It says signature, Mister Quillon. Not soul. Not contract. I read it. Mum always says read the small print.
Mister Quillon: Your mother is a very tiresome woman.
Narrator: The contract in his ledger begins to smoke at one corner. Then the whole page catches, with a soft, polite sound, and burns down to his gloves, and he doesn't drop it.
Mister Quillon: The terms are met. The account is closed. Good evening, Mister Smudgewick. I do hope we never meet again. I will make sure we don't.
Narrator: Then he isn't there, and neither is the paper with Strahd's name on it.
if flag.strahd_letter_kept and not flag.kip_strahd_signed and not flag.kip_invitation_kept
set strahd_letter_kept = false
endif
set kip_contract_void
quest the_fine_print free
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Is that... is that it? Is it done? I don't feel any different. I thought I'd feel different. I thought there'd be a noise.
Narrator: Then Kip takes an apricot out of his pocket, from habit, and looks at it, and makes a noise. There's a soft brown bruise on one side of it. It wasn't there this morning.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: It's going off. It's going off! LOOK. It's going brown! Oh, thank every god there is, it's ROTTING.
interject name:thistle: Why's he crying at a bad apricot?
interject name:wren_featherfoot: Long story. Good ending.
inspire name:kip_smudgewick: read the small print, like Mum always said
if approval.kip_smudgewick >= close
Narrator: Later, when the others are asleep, Kip sits up by the fire with his little knife and the stone of the bruised apricot, carving. In the morning there's a charm on a string, tied to the pack of whoever slept nearest.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Apricot-pit charm. Finest in three counties. I've sold hundreds, and not one of them ever worked, because the orchard was cursed.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: This one might. First pit off a free tree. No charge. Partners don't charge each other.
give stone_of_good_luck
endif
approve kip_smudgewick +8: You stood by me while I settled up with Mister Quillon
-> END

~ talk_home
if name:kip_smudgewick and quest.the_fine_print >= free and flag.eva_truth_teller != "kip_smudgewick"
-> home
endif
-> END

~ home
Narrator: Kip is writing a letter by the fire, slowly, crossing out more than he writes.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: It's to my dad. I'm not going to be able to send it. There's no post out of here. I'm writing it anyway.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: He sent Mister Quillon away from the gate. That winter. Told him we'd manage. And I went after him down the lane, and signed, and came home and said the trees had come back on their own. A miracle. He went to the temple and gave thanks for it.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: I've been lying to my dad for two years, every day, every time an apricot didn't rot.
* You saved them. -> home_saved
* You can tell him when you get home. -> home_tell

~ home_saved
interject name:kip_smudgewick: I did. I did do that. I keep forgetting that part. I'm going to put it in the letter. "I'm sorry, and also I saved everyone." That's a good sentence. That's a sentence a person can live with.
approve kip_smudgewick +2: You reminded me I saved them
-> END

~ home_tell
interject name:kip_smudgewick: When I get home. Yes. When I get home I'll tell him, and he'll be furious, and then he'll make me dig the whole bottom field on my own, and I'll be so happy I'll cry into the soil.
approve kip_smudgewick +2: You told me I'd get home to tell Dad myself
-> END

# --- As he comes to trust the party (approval) --------------------------------------------------------------------

~ talk_warm
if name:kip_smudgewick and approval.kip_smudgewick >= warm and level >= 3
-> warm
endif
-> END

~ warm
Narrator: Kip is carving tonight: an apricot-pit charm, turning it under his thumb as the little knife goes round. There's a row of them on the log beside him, each face slightly different.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Finest apricot-pit charms in three counties. I'd say four, but there's a woman in the next valley who does a very good rabbit.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Pip used to sit with me and choose the faces. She liked them sad. Said a sad one would understand you better. She's little. She's very wise. Gets it from her grandmother. Not from me.
* Tell us about Pip. -> warm_pip
* Can I have one? -> warm_charm
* Which one's the sad one? -> warm_sad

~ warm_pip
interject name:kip_smudgewick: She sells better than me. She stands on a crate at market and tells people the apricots are sad to be left behind. They buy two. She's going to run the stall one day, and I'll just carry the boxes. That's my whole retirement plan.
approve kip_smudgewick +3: You asked me about Pip
-> END

~ warm_charm
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Course. One apricot. No. No charge. Don't tell my mum; she says I give the stock away.
approve kip_smudgewick +2: You asked for one of my charms
-> END

~ warm_sad
Narrator: He holds one up. It's a very small face with its mouth turned down and its eyebrows up, the way a person looks when they've been told bad news kindly.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: That one. I did that one in the Svalich woods, the first night. I didn't mean to. My hands just did it.
approve kip_smudgewick +2: You asked which one was the sad one
-> END

~ talk_close
if name:kip_smudgewick and approval.kip_smudgewick >= close and level >= 5
-> close
endif
-> END

~ close
Narrator: Kip is sitting a little way from the fire with his hat off. Without it his horns look very large and very red in the firelight, and he keeps touching one of them, the way some people touch a scar.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: People see the horns and count their change twice. All my life. I don't blame them. I'd count mine, if I were them and I saw me.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: So I've always been honest. Stupidly honest. Change counted out loud. Weights on the table where you can see them. Because if I wasn't, I'd only be what they thought I was.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: And then I signed that paper in the lane. And now I don't know if I'm the honest one or the horns.
* You're the honest one. -> close_honest
* The horns don't decide that, Kip. You do. -> close_decide
* We've never once counted our change around you. -> close_change

~ close_honest
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Am I? Even after? ...You'd know. You've seen me at it. You've seen me give back the change people didn't notice.
approve kip_smudgewick +3: You told me I'm the honest one
-> close_end

~ close_decide
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Me. Huh. That's a frightening amount of responsibility. I'll need it in writing. No, I won't. I'll need it said again sometime, that's all.
approve kip_smudgewick +3: You told me I decide who I am, not the horns
-> close_end

~ close_change
interject name:kip_smudgewick: You haven't, have you. I'd have noticed. I always notice.
approve kip_smudgewick +3: You told me you've never counted your change around me
-> close_end

~ close_end
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Right. Good. Lovely. I'm putting my hat back on now, because it's cold, not for any emotional reason. Apricot? They're all free tonight.
-> END

~ talk_devoted
if name:kip_smudgewick and approval.kip_smudgewick >= devoted and level >= 8
-> devoted
endif
-> END

~ devoted
Narrator: Kip has unrolled a fresh sheet of paper on the log and is writing, very carefully, in his best market-day hand. When you lean over, he lets you look. It's headed PARTNERSHIP AGREEMENT.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Smudgewick and Company. Apricots and adventuring. I've put all your names in. I've left the small print blank on purpose. No small print. Just the big print.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Clause one: nobody signs anything without reading it. Clause two: nobody goes into the dark alone. Clause three: I bring the apricots. There isn't a clause four. Three's a good number for clauses.
* Where do I sign? -> devoted_sign
* What's in it for you? -> devoted_what

~ devoted_sign
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Just there. Freely given, in your own hand. ...I've wanted to say that to someone and mean it nicely for two years.
approve kip_smudgewick +3: You signed Smudgewick and Company's partnership agreement
-> devoted_end

~ devoted_what
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Company. That's all. That's the whole consideration. It's a very good deal. Best I ever made.
approve kip_smudgewick +3: You asked what was in it for me, and let me say it
-> devoted_end

~ devoted_end
inspire name:kip_smudgewick: drew up a contract with no small print
-> END
